--- a/DESIGN.md
+++ b/DESIGN.md
@@ -417,7 +417,7 @@ One hourglass (`TensionClock` in `SceneManager.jsx`, drawn by `Hourglass` in `sh
 - **Table strip** (`shared/TableStrip.jsx`): one slim parchment band with the tension (hourglass mark, n/4), the running timer in mono (oxblood at 0:00, "(paused)" when paused) and the newest Activity Log line as the log shows it (the server's lines in the rulebook's words, chat as written), italic and cut off with an ellipsis. It draws nothing when the tension is 0, no timer shows and there is no log line. It sits where the hourglass itself is out of sight: under a phone's band (held with it, except on the Hourglass page), at the top of a tablet's page and over the Notebook at every size, and on the Lightkeeper's desk across the top of the desk whenever the roster is not showing (an open sheet, the Circle, the Notebook, the Map or the Requests), and under a phone's band as on the player's (it once fell to the foot of the page, after them and the dice). From xl the player's rail shows the hourglass, so the strip steps aside there except over the Notebook. It is not a live region: a ticking timer would be read out every second.
 - **"New" dots** (`NewDot`): a 10 px oxblood dot with a cream rim and "(new)" for screen readers, on the Menu button, on the drawer rows (From the Lightkeeper, Hourglass, Circle) and on the md+ Circle tab. The store keeps `unseen` for a new dispatch, a moved tension or timer and reports opening; a part's dot goes when that part is in front of the player (from md the dispatch and hourglass stand on the desk, so theirs clear at once). Nothing is marked when the desk first loads a circle.
 - **Rail and log at laptop heights**: the player's left rail scrolls as a whole from xl, Your Circle keeps a floor of 7.5 rem, the log keeps 9 rem, and the dice column scrolls inside itself, so the timer and Pass Notes are always reachable. On a tablet the rail follows the dice in the page, so reading order matches what is drawn.
-- **Gilded choice**: under "Keep one die" the slip lists what each die would do ("Keep the gilded 4: Mixed success, 1 Nerve back" / "Keep the 4: Mixed success"), the dice and the phone bar's buttons carry the same words, and after a gilded keep the slip says "1 Nerve back". Each line on the slip is a button doing what its die does (44 px high). When the drive is already full the gilded line drops ", 1 X back", since nothing would come back, and the log carries no "refreshed" tag then.
+- **Gilded choice**: under "Keep one die" the slip lists what each die would do ("Keep the gilded 4: Mixed success, 1 Nerve back" / "Keep the 4: Mixed success"), the phone bar's buttons carry the same words (the dice themselves are hidden from assistive tech and leave the tab order, so each choice is announced once, from its line; a die stays clickable and stops shimmying on hover), and after a gilded keep the slip says "1 Nerve back". Each line on the slip is a button doing what its die does (44 px high). When the drive is already full the gilded line drops ", 1 X back", since nothing would come back, and the log carries no "refreshed" tag then.
 - **Circle resources**: a player spends with one button per resource that asks first (armed, it reads "Spend", with "Press again to spend 1 Refresh for the circle." under it) and stays armed until the player presses elsewhere, presses Escape or moves focus away, like every other confirm (a four-second lapse cost every player of the third playtest a spend); a second press within half a second of the first is the rest of a double-click and is ignored; the "Press again" line under each button keeps its space while spending is open and is shown only on the armed one, so arming or disarming never moves a button under the finger; the pips are display only for players, the reason spending is blocked is printed above the buttons, and the counter reads "Your spends n / 2". The Lightkeeper keeps the pips and, on a member's sheet, "Give a spend back".
 
 ### Formation papers on the Lightkeeper's desk (2026-10-10, playtest)
